<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mandatory Palestine</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1948 CE · capital Jerusalem</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British-ruled mandate territory of Palestine, where the conflict between Jewish and Arab national movements took shape.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/occupied-enemy-territory-administration">Occupied Enemy Territory Administration</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> set up a civil government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-samuel">Herbert Samuel</a> in July 1920. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>, approved in 1922, incorporated the 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration">Balfour Declaration's</a> promise of a Jewish national home, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-transjordan">Transjordan</a> was administered separately.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A high commissioner ruled without an elected legislature. Jewish immigration, land purchase and institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jewish-agency-for-israel">Jewish Agency</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histadrut">Histadrut</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yishuv">Yishuv</a>, while Arab leaders opposed the national home. Violence erupted in 1920, 1921 and 1929, and the Arab revolt of 1936-1939 led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peel-commission">Peel Commission</a> to propose partition. The 1939 White Paper limited Jewish immigration, and after 1944 Jewish groups fought the British.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> referred the question to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, which approved partition in November 1947. The mandate ended on 14-15 May 1948, when the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Israel</a> was declared and war with Arab states followed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mandatory-palestine.jpg" alt="Mandatory Palestine" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jerusalem</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herbert Samuel, Arthur Wauchope, Alan Cunningham</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam, Judaism, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Hebrew, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Mandate for Palestine, Palestine (mandate)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=mandatory-palestine" aria-label="Mandatory Palestine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5588 1021 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mandatory Palestine lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5908 1277L5914 1207M5908 1277L4997 651M5908 1277L4997 651M5908 1277L5925 1271M5908 1277L5908 1277M5908 1277L5907 1273"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5914 1207h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M5925 1271h0M5908 1277h0M5907 1273h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jerusalem. <a href="/atlas?year=1934&amp;focus=mandatory-palestine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
